WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.474
Deploying and training neural networks on edge
devices is often bottlenecked by tight memory.

00:00:06.554 --> 00:00:11.877
Tensor rematerialization, recomputing intermediate
tensors instead of storing them,

00:00:11.957 --> 00:00:14.676
trades extra compute for lower peak memory.

00:00:14.756 --> 00:00:16.694
This paper introduces Moccasin,

00:00:16.774 --> 00:00:23.724
a new constraint programming formulation for the
problem of minimizing execution time under a memory budget.

00:00:23.804 --> 00:00:28.216
Unlike prior work that needs a quadratic number of
Boolean variables,

00:00:28.296 --> 00:00:31.861
Moccasin uses only a linear number of integer variables,

00:00:31.941 --> 00:00:37.720
letting it scale to much larger compute graphs and
run up to an order of magnitude faster.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:43.021
Neural networks running on edge devices are
constrained above all by memory.

00:00:43.101 --> 00:00:48.811
One way to fit a large model into a small memory
footprint is rematerialization: instead

00:00:48.891 --> 00:00:51.246
of storing every intermediate tensor,

00:00:51.326 --> 00:00:53.681
you recompute some of them on demand.

00:00:53.761 --> 00:00:56.510
The catch is that recomputation costs time.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:02.630
So the core problem is a scheduling one: for a
given compute graph and a fixed memory budget,

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:08.223
decide which tensors to retain and which to
recompute so that total execution time is

00:01:08.303 --> 00:01:12.434
as small as possible while peak memory never
exceeds the budget.

00:01:12.514 --> 00:01:17.040
This is a hard combinatorial optimization, in
general PSPACE-complete.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.564
The leading prior method, Checkmate,

00:01:21.644 --> 00:01:25.789
casts rematerialization as a mixed-integer linear program.

00:01:25.869 --> 00:01:30.013
It is expressive,

00:01:30.093 --> 00:01:34.238
but its number of Boolean decision variables grows
with the square of the number of nodes in the graph.

00:01:34.318 --> 00:01:38.462
That quadratic growth becomes a wall: for graphs
with a few hundred nodes and a few thousand edges,

00:01:38.542 --> 00:01:42.687
Checkmate either times out or runs out of memory
during the solve.

00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:46.911
Because the trend toward on-device training and
stricter latency targets keeps enlarging these graphs,

00:01:46.991 --> 00:01:51.136
we need a formulation whose complexity grows much
more slowly,

00:01:51.216 --> 00:01:55.360
so realistic graphs can be solved within an
acceptable compile time.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.117
The paper's main contribution is Moccasin,

00:02:00.197 --> 00:02:04.574
a constraint programming formulation of tensor
rematerialization.

00:02:04.654 --> 00:02:09.031
Its central idea is to represent each node's
decisions as a small number of retention intervals,

00:02:09.111 --> 00:02:13.488
defined by the start and end event of the tensor's
lifetime,

00:02:13.568 --> 00:02:17.945
which reduces the count of discrete variables from
quadratic to linear in the number of nodes.

00:02:18.025 --> 00:02:22.402
On top of this,

00:02:22.482 --> 00:02:26.859
the authors show how to encode the nonlinear memory
and precedence constraints using standard CP building blocks,

00:02:26.939 --> 00:02:31.316
the cumulative and reservoir constraints,

00:02:31.396 --> 00:02:35.773
and they add an optional variant that enforces an
input topological ordering to further reduce the search space.

00:02:35.853 --> 00:02:40.230
The payoff is up to an order-of-magnitude speedup
over prior work on large graphs.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:44.161
Moccasin represents the schedule with retention intervals.

00:02:44.241 --> 00:02:45.851
For each node in the graph,

00:02:45.931 --> 00:02:49.669
the method allows up to a small constant number of
intervals,

00:02:49.749 --> 00:02:53.801
each specified by an integer start event and an
integer end event,

00:02:53.881 --> 00:02:59.184
indicating when that node's output tensor is
computed and how long it stays in memory.

00:02:59.264 --> 00:03:03.065
A Boolean flag marks whether each interval is
actually active,

00:03:03.145 --> 00:03:06.633
so a node need not be recomputed a fixed number of times.

00:03:06.713 --> 00:03:09.325
The objective minimizes the total duration,

00:03:09.405 --> 00:03:14.896
a weighted sum over the active compute events where
the weights are node execution times.

00:03:14.976 --> 00:03:20.342
The memory budget is enforced through the
constraint programming cumulative constraint,

00:03:20.422 --> 00:03:24.661
treating tensor sizes as resource demands and the
budget as capacity,

00:03:24.741 --> 00:03:31.046
while data dependencies between predecessors and
successors are handled with the reservoir constraint.

00:03:31.126 --> 00:03:34.802
The whole model is handed to Google OR-Tools'
CP-SAT solver.

00:03:34.882 --> 00:03:38.871
Because each node uses only a small constant number
of intervals,

00:03:38.951 --> 00:03:43.190
the total number of integer variables stays linear
in the graph size.

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:46.438
The evaluation uses a range of compute graphs.

00:03:46.518 --> 00:03:50.989
The main scaling study is on four random layered
graphs, G1 through G4,

00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:56.437
whose sizes grow from one hundred nodes and a
couple hundred edges up to one thousand

00:03:56.517 --> 00:03:58.744
nodes and nearly six thousand edges.

00:03:58.824 --> 00:04:04.512
There is also a real-world compute graph with four
hundred forty-two nodes and over twelve

00:04:04.592 --> 00:04:07.525
hundred edges used for the headline comparison.

00:04:07.605 --> 00:04:13.165
For every graph the memory budget is set to eighty
and ninety percent of the peak memory

00:04:13.245 --> 00:04:16.369
of the initial schedule without rematerialization,

00:04:16.449 --> 00:04:19.830
so the solver is forced to actually recompute tensors.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:25.150
All experiments run on a sixteen-core workstation
with thirty-two gigabytes of RAM.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:29.379
Across the board,

00:04:29.459 --> 00:04:33.308
Moccasin solves the rematerialization problem
substantially faster than Checkmate,

00:04:33.388 --> 00:04:37.237
up to an order of magnitude on the larger graphs.

00:04:37.317 --> 00:04:41.166
On the smallest graph the two are comparable, but
the gap widens quickly.

00:04:41.246 --> 00:04:45.095
For the graph with two hundred fifty nodes and a
tight memory budget,

00:04:45.175 --> 00:04:49.025
Checkmate fails to find any feasible solution
within thirty minutes,

00:04:49.105 --> 00:04:52.954
and at the loosest budget it takes ten minutes,
while Moccasin finishes in seconds.

00:04:53.034 --> 00:04:56.883
For the five hundred and one thousand node graphs,
Checkmate times out entirely,

00:04:56.963 --> 00:05:00.812
finding no solution even given three hours, and it
exits with an out-of-memory error,

00:05:00.892 --> 00:05:04.741
whereas Moccasin converges to a good,

00:05:04.821 --> 00:05:08.670
low-duration-increase solution in under an hour.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:11.127
The paper studies a few key knobs.

00:05:11.207 --> 00:05:16.647
The most important is the optional
topological-ordering restriction: enforcing an input

00:05:16.727 --> 00:05:21.976
ordering enlarges the variable domain slightly but
shrinks the overall search space,

00:05:22.056 --> 00:05:26.227
reducing solve time compared to the fully
unrestricted formulation.

00:05:26.307 --> 00:05:31.493
The per-node interval budget, called C, is fixed at
two throughout the experiments,

00:05:31.573 --> 00:05:34.285
which the authors note in every plot legend.

00:05:34.365 --> 00:05:39.995
The complexity table makes the contrast concrete:
Checkmate's Boolean variable count grows

00:05:40.075 --> 00:05:43.738
quadratically in the number of nodes plus a
node-edge term,

00:05:43.818 --> 00:05:49.004
while Moccasin's integer variable count grows only
linearly in the number of nodes,

00:05:49.084 --> 00:05:50.590
with a constant factor C.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:54.812
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:05:54.892 --> 00:05:58.734
Moccasin needs only a linear number of integer
variables in the graph size,

00:05:58.814 --> 00:06:02.656
compared to a quadratic number of Boolean variables
for the prior state of the art.

00:06:02.736 --> 00:06:06.578
This translates into solve times up to an order of
magnitude, roughly ten times,

00:06:06.658 --> 00:06:10.500
faster on large graphs.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:14.422
And the solutions it finds are high quality: the
total duration increase from rematerialization

00:06:14.502 --> 00:06:18.344
stays consistently below five percent.

00:06:18.424 --> 00:06:22.266
Crucially,

00:06:22.346 --> 00:06:26.188
Moccasin scales to graphs with up to one thousand
nodes and nearly six thousand edges,

00:06:26.268 --> 00:06:30.110
a regime where the competing method simply times
out or runs out of memory.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:35.883
The lasting takeaway is that the right problem
formulation changes what is tractable.

00:06:35.963 --> 00:06:42.580
By expressing rematerialization decisions as a
small set of retention intervals in a constraint program,

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:48.568
Moccasin reduces the number of discrete variables
from quadratic to linear in the graph size.

00:06:48.648 --> 00:06:54.621
That single change lets it scale to compute graphs
far larger than prior methods could handle,

00:06:54.701 --> 00:07:00.802
solving them up to ten times faster while keeping
the added runtime overhead under five percent.

00:07:00.882 --> 00:07:04.215
And because the output is just an execution sequence,

00:07:04.295 --> 00:07:07.370
the resulting schedule can run on any CPU or GPU.
